# Decimal vs double

Money and catalog prices are `decimal`. Binary `double` literals and NUnit `[TestCase]` numbers are not exact decimals. On modern .NET, `(decimal)doubleValue` / `Convert.ToDecimal(double)` keep full binary precision and break equality checks.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD: TestCase doubles cast straight to decimal
[TestCase(12.37, 12.37)]
public void CanRound(decimal value, decimal expected) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD: accept doubles from TestCase, convert with 15 significant digits
[TestCase(12.37, 12.37)]
public void CanRound(double value, double expected)
{
    Round(ToDecimal(value)).Should().Be(ToDecimal(expected));
}

static decimal ToDecimal(double value)
{
    Span<char> text = stackalloc char[32];
    value.TryFormat(text, out var length, "G15", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
    return decimal.Parse(text[..length], NumberStyles.Float, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
}
```

Prefer `12.37m` decimal literals in production code. When bridging unavoidable `double` math (for example `Math.Pow` for package cube roots), use the same `G15` conversion if product behavior depended on pre-.NET 11 rounding—do not leave a bare `Convert.ToDecimal(Math.Pow(...))`.
